#doc (Manual) "Interactive Schnorr in Rust" =>

This prototype proves conditional refinement and perfect honest-verifier
zero-knowledge for an extracted Rust Schnorr protocol. Its arithmetic interface
has explicit contracts over every valid native scalar and point value.
No concrete curve implementation is proved here. An
[executable Ristretto adapter](source/Schnorr/rust/native/src/lib.rs.html) exercises
the same Rust trait in tests; those tests do not discharge the arithmetic contracts.

[Read the Rust program](source/Schnorr/rust/src/lib.rs.html) ·
[Read the extraction script](source/Schnorr/scripts/extract.sh.html) ·
[Read the proof](source/Schnorr/lean/Schnorr/Security.lean.html)

# Protocol and executed code

Let $`F` be a field, $`G` an additive commutative group with an $`F`-module
structure, and $`g\in G`. The witness is $`x\in F`; its public statement is
$`X=x\mathbin{\bullet}g`. The prover samples a nonce $`r`, sends
$`R=r\mathbin{\bullet}g`, receives challenge $`c`, and replies with
$`z=r+cx`. The verifier accepts exactly when
$`z\mathbin{\bullet}g=R+c\mathbin{\bullet}X`.

The Rust `Group` trait supplies six arithmetic operations. `commit`, `respond`,
and `verify` are deterministic. `messages` computes the two prover messages
from an explicit nonce and challenge. A live exchange must send the commitment
before it receives the challenge. This helper does not enforce network order.

::::definition "program" (lean := "schnorr_core.messages")
:::SchnorrBlueprint.pair schnorr_core.messages -docstrings
This declaration is generated by Charon and Aeneas from the Rust `messages`
function. The refinement proof unfolds this declaration. The sampled experiment
calls extracted `commit` and `respond` in interactive order; `rustTranscript_eq_batch`
identifies its result with a sampled call to this helper.
:::
::::

# Arithmetic boundary

The model contains native scalar and point types $`S,P`, mathematical encoders
and decoders, with decode-after-encode roundtrip laws. Each primitive contract
holds for every value of its native input types and returns `Result.ok`.
Every native value has a decoded meaning; representations need not be unique.
Each scalar or point output must nevertheless equal the chosen `M.point` or
`M.scalar` encoding, not merely decode to the right mathematical value.
A future projective or dalek backend may need normalization or a relation-based
refinement to meet an appropriate contract. The tested Ristretto adapter is
not known to satisfy this exact-output model. Byte parsing
and rejection of invalid wire data require a separate interface and proof.

::::definition "contracts" (lean := "SchnorrProof.PrimitiveModel")
:::SchnorrBlueprint.pair SchnorrProof.PrimitiveModel -docstrings
`PrimitiveModel` states all arithmetic and representation assumptions.
The theorem is conditional on this model. It neither constructs a dalek
instance nor proves a concrete backend satisfies these contracts.
:::
::::

::::theorem "commit_contract" (lean := "SchnorrProof.commit_eq") (uses := "contracts")
:::SchnorrBlueprint.pair SchnorrProof.commit_eq -docstrings
The extracted commitment returns the encoding of $`r\mathbin{\bullet}g`.
:::
::::

::::theorem "respond_contract" (lean := "SchnorrProof.respond_eq") (uses := "contracts")
:::SchnorrBlueprint.pair SchnorrProof.respond_eq -docstrings
The extracted response returns the encoding of $`r+cx`.
:::
::::

::::theorem "messages" (lean := "SchnorrProof.messages_eq") (uses := "program, commit_contract, respond_contract")
:::SchnorrBlueprint.pair SchnorrProof.messages_eq -docstrings
For all $`x,r,c\in F`, the extracted prover returns the encoded pair
$`(r\mathbin{\bullet}g,r+cx)` successfully.
:::
::::

::::theorem "verify" (lean := "SchnorrProof.verify_accepts_iff") (uses := "contracts")
:::SchnorrBlueprint.pair SchnorrProof.verify_accepts_iff -docstrings
For arbitrary native $`X,R:P` and $`c,z:S`, acceptance is equivalent to the
Schnorr equation on decoded values. These inputs need not come from an honest
prover. The underlying `verify_eq` theorem also identifies the full
`Result Bool` value, including rejection.
:::
::::

# Completeness and special soundness

::::theorem "complete" (lean := "SchnorrProof.honest_complete") (uses := "verify")
:::SchnorrBlueprint.pair SchnorrProof.honest_complete -docstrings
Every honest transcript verifies for every nonce and challenge.
The proof uses the module identities
$`(r+cx)\mathbin{\bullet}g=r\mathbin{\bullet}g+c\mathbin{\bullet}(x\mathbin{\bullet}g)`.
:::
::::

::::theorem "sound" (lean := "SchnorrProof.special_soundness") (uses := "verify")
:::SchnorrBlueprint.pair SchnorrProof.special_soundness -docstrings
Suppose two native transcripts share the public statement and commitment,
both verify, and their decoded challenges differ. Then
$`(z_1-z_2)(c_1-c_2)^{-1}` is a witness for the decoded public statement.
The proof applies VCVio's `Schnorr.sigma_speciallySound` after verifier refinement.
:::
::::

# Sampled transcripts and perfect HVZK

The experiment samples independent ideal scalars in `ProbComp`, encodes them,
and passes them to deterministic extracted functions. The challenge law is
that of an honest verifier. The simulator samples independent $`c,z` and sets
$`R=z\mathbin{\bullet}g-c\mathbin{\bullet}X` without the witness.

::::definition "experiment" (lean := "SchnorrProof.rustTranscript")
:::SchnorrBlueprint.pair SchnorrProof.rustTranscript -docstrings
The sampled experiment executes extracted `commit` before it samples the
challenge and calls extracted `respond`. It retains the extracted `Result`
rather than replacing failure with a default transcript. Inspect this definition
for the sample order, encoding, extracted calls, and returned transcript.
:::
::::

::::theorem "batch" (lean := "SchnorrProof.rustTranscript_eq_batch") (uses := "messages, commit_contract, respond_contract, experiment")
:::SchnorrBlueprint.pair SchnorrProof.rustTranscript_eq_batch -docstrings
The interactive experiment also equals the sampled extracted `messages` helper
under the primitive contracts. This identifies both entry paths without moving
the challenge before the commitment in the interactive definition.
:::
::::

::::theorem "simulator" (lean := "SchnorrProof.encodedSimulator_eq")
:::SchnorrBlueprint.pair SchnorrProof.encodedSimulator_eq -docstrings
The simulator samples challenge and response, then reconstructs the commitment
from the verification equation. Its input is the public statement alone.
:::
::::

::::theorem "transport" (lean := "SchnorrProof.rustTranscript_eq_encodedReal") (uses := "commit_contract, respond_contract, experiment")
:::SchnorrBlueprint.pair SchnorrProof.rustTranscript_eq_encodedReal -docstrings
The native experiment equals the encoded VCVio real experiment. This equality
preserves the complete `Result` value, not only successful outputs.
:::
::::

::::theorem "hvzk" (lean := "SchnorrProof.perfect_hvzk") (uses := "transport, simulator")
:::SchnorrBlueprint.pair SchnorrProof.perfect_hvzk -docstrings
For a valid witness, the native real transcript and encoded simulator have
identical distributions. The proof reuses VCVio's `Schnorr.sigma_hvzk`.
The distinguishing advantage is therefore zero for every test of these
transcripts. The arithmetic contracts and ideal sampling law remain assumptions.
:::
::::

# Stateful randomness and oracle simulation

The Rust `ScalarRng` trait and `commit_random` wrapper expose one stateful
sample followed by commitment. `commitmentProgram` describes that request as
an oracle effect. `simulateQ` gives the same program two separate interpretations.

::::theorem "rng_runtime" (lean := "SchnorrProof.Randomness.runtime_commitment_eq")
:::SchnorrBlueprint.pair SchnorrProof.Randomness.runtime_commitment_eq -docstrings
The stateful interpretation equals extracted `commit_random` for every arithmetic
interface, RNG implementation, and initial state. It preserves failure,
divergence, and the final RNG state on success. No sampling-law premise is used.
:::
::::

::::theorem "rng_ideal" (lean := "SchnorrProof.Randomness.ideal_commitment_refines") (uses := "commit_contract")
:::SchnorrBlueprint.pair SchnorrProof.Randomness.ideal_commitment_refines -docstrings
The ideal interpretation uses a fresh uniform scalar for each request.
Under the arithmetic contracts, the resulting commitment is its encoded scalar
multiple of the basepoint. This is a different handler from the runtime RNG.
:::
::::

These two theorems do not equate the runtime and ideal output laws.
A monad lift or an oracle handler's type does not establish its sampling law.
A native RNG security theorem would need a relation on states and a distributional
correspondence theorem for each call, preserved across the whole execution.
The final HVZK theorem above uses the explicit ideal nonce and challenge model.

[BAIF PR1 Common/Correspondence](https://github.com/Beneficial-AI-Foundation/bridging-examples/blob/070b07d98275ab949e769965a2bf29eaac109f51/Common/Correspondence.lean)
provides `bridge_of_spec` and `bind_call` for a compositional bridge between
program calls and specifications. Such a bridge transports established contracts;
it does not supply an entropy assumption.

[SPQR Adapter](https://github.com/Beneficial-AI-Foundation/spqr-sm-bridge/blob/551dca1a33b659a930a981fbfa02e84b528503a8/SpqrBridge/Adapter.lean)
and [TapeFacts](https://github.com/Beneficial-AI-Foundation/spqr-sm-bridge/blob/551dca1a33b659a930a981fbfa02e84b528503a8/SpqrBridge/TapeFacts.lean)
use a random tape to separate deterministic execution from probabilistic input.
Its [README](https://github.com/Beneficial-AI-Foundation/spqr-sm-bridge/blob/551dca1a33b659a930a981fbfa02e84b528503a8/README.md)
states an ideal uniform-byte model. That model does not prove a real RNG law.
Here the explicit inputs are scalars, so there is no byte-to-scalar distribution
claim either.

# Scope and reproduction

These results concern interactive Schnorr completeness, special soundness, and
perfect honest-verifier zero-knowledge. They do not prove Fiat–Shamir signatures,
EUF-CMA security, malicious-verifier zero-knowledge, wire parsing, compiled
machine behavior, side-channel resistance, or concrete arithmetic primitives.
No discrete-log hardness assumption is needed for the three stated properties.
No running-time bound is proved for arithmetic, representation maps, the
extractor, or the simulator. Perfect HVZK denotes exact transcript-distribution
equality here, without a proved efficiency bound for a concrete simulator.
The proofs use the field and module laws; they do not assume a bijection
between scalars and group elements.

